  {
   "id": "2101657635875627341",
   "url": "https://x.com/billhpicks/status/2101657635875627341",
   "text": "Hammering This Over\ud83d\udd28\n\nTrey McBride Over 60.5 Receiving Yards\n\n80% Hit Rate Over L5 \ud83c\udfaf\n\n75\u2764\ufe0f For My NFL Play Of The Day\n\nMcBride has cleared this number in 4 of his last 5 games, averaging 79.4 receiving yards over that stretch. He opened 2026 with 9 catches on 13 targets for 95 yards and a touchdown at the Chargers, a 34% share of Arizona's targets in a 26-14 win. He has been a problem for Seattle throughout his career, totaling 579 yards over eight games against them for 72.4 per game, including 127 yards and a touchdown on 13 targets in the last meeting. He was removed from the injury report and will play, while Seattle is without safety Ty Okada and has Nick Emmanwori questionable. \n\nLast Season Trey McBride Averaged.\n\n-72.9 Receiving Yards Per Game\n-9.9 Targets Per Game\n-7.3 Yards Per Target\n-24% Team Target Share\n\nSeattle ranks 7th in the league in receiving yards allowed to tight ends, giving up 28 per game, which makes this a tougher matchup for the over on paper. That number comes from a single Week 1 game against New England.\n\nLast 5 TEs Vs Seattle.\n\n-Hunter Henry 26 Receiving Yards\n-Hunter Henry 31 Receiving Yards\n-Colby Parkinson 62 Receiving Yards\n-Jake Tonges 59 Receiving Yards\n-George Kittle 29 Receiving Yards\n\nThe number is fair, and it sits below the 72.9 yards per game McBride averaged last season, given how Arizona uses him: 13 targets and a 34% target share in Week 1 after 9.9 targets per game a year ago. Seattle has held tight ends in check, as only one of the last five top tight ends to face them cleared 60.5, but none of that group has McBride's role, and he has done his damage against this defense before. At last season's 9.8 yards per catch he needs six or seven receptions to get there, and he averaged 7.4 catches per game. The over hits if he sees a workload close to his usual 10 targets.\n\nThe history against Seattle helps too. McBride has three games of at least 70 receiving yards in his last four against the Seahawks, including two 100-yard performances. Seattle's defense is strong overall, allowing the eighth-fewest passing yards in the NFL at 168 per game, but McBride's targets come from how Arizona builds its passing game, not from how open he gets. With Seattle's secondary short a safety and Arizona's offense running through him, another 8 or more targets should get him past 61 yards.",
   "author_handle": "billhpicks",
   "author_name": "Bill",
   "author_followers": 29992,
   "created_at": "2026-09-20T13:00:01+00:00",
